# Line-ending policy.
#
# Why this file exists: the golden and pipeline fixtures are compared **byte for
# byte** against freshly generated output, and the encoding policy they pin is
# UTF-8 with LF and no BOM. The assertions are in
#   tests/golden/test_golden_fixtures.py::test_golden_files_follow_the_encoding_policy
#   tests/golden/test_golden_fixtures.py::test_every_project_fixture_matches_its_recipe
#   tests/golden/test_af3_pipeline_fixtures.py::test_committed_fixtures_match_a_fresh_run
#
# Without a policy, `core.autocrlf=true` -- the default on this machine -- writes
# CRLF into a fresh Windows checkout. The fixtures are then no longer the bytes
# the encoder emits, and those tests fail in the clone while passing in the
# checkout that produced them. That is exactly what happened: three of them
# failed in real `git clone` output before this file existed.
#
# `-text` on the byte-exact fixtures disables all end-of-line conversion, so the
# bytes in the repository are the bytes on disk on every platform. It is applied
# only where a test compares bytes; the rest of the tree keeps `text=auto` so the
# usual commit-time normalisation still applies.

# Default: normalise on commit, and do not gain CR on checkout.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Byte-exact fixtures. A later matching line wins, so these come after the
# default above.
#
#   fixtures/golden/**             committed golden.json vs a fresh encode
#   tests/fixtures/af3_pipeline/** committed documents vs a fresh generation
#
# Note: recipe.json under fixtures/golden/ is covered by the same rule. It is
# not compared byte-for-byte, but it is authored beside golden.json and 2-byte
# line endings in an authored fixture are not worth the inconsistency.
fixtures/golden/**        -text
tests/fixtures/af3_pipeline/**  -text

# Binary assets, should any be added later.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.gz   binary
*.tar  binary
*.zip  binary
